WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.765
Hi, I'm Mambo!

00:00:02.765 --> 00:00:06.634
A research harness turns one photo into an editable Blender program.

00:00:06.634 --> 00:00:09.800
Here's how, and which parts a stronger model eats.

00:00:11.142 --> 00:00:15.705
Rebuild the scene the way an artist works: geometry, material, composition, then

00:00:15.705 --> 00:00:18.366
lighting, each closed by its own verifier.

00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:24.051
From one photo, the model sketches four rough scenes.

00:00:24.051 --> 00:00:26.449
Every object gets a stable Blender name.

00:00:26.869 --> 00:00:29.677
A selector keeps the one with the most complete coverage.

00:00:30.097 --> 00:00:34.173
Then the four stages, each with a round budget: five for geometry,

00:00:34.173 --> 00:00:37.230
three each for material and composition, two for lighting.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:41.237
Each stage commits before the next begins, so every step is

00:00:41.237 --> 00:00:42.541
an editable Blender program.

00:00:44.192 --> 00:00:48.115
Inside a stage, the generator edits through a tool scoped to it.

00:00:48.115 --> 00:00:50.731
The material tool can only make material edits.

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:54.913
Blender renders the result, and the verifier returns a checklist rather

00:00:54.913 --> 00:00:56.281
than a free critique.

00:00:56.701 --> 00:00:58.249
Once approved, the stage commits.

00:00:58.249 --> 00:01:00.107
No later stage may undo it.

00:01:01.508 --> 00:01:05.683
Staging was measured on one model: plus one point two five decibels.

00:01:05.683 --> 00:01:10.206
The revisit says the gain nearly vanishes on stronger models, with no number.

00:01:11.817 --> 00:01:15.966
A substitute covers something the model can't do yet, like the staging itself.

00:01:15.966 --> 00:01:16.923
Scaling eats it.

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:20.170
Supply brings in a fact from outside, like the render.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:23.574
A constraint forbids an outcome, like the material-only tool.

00:01:23.574 --> 00:01:27.220
It isn't eaten, but what it gives up grows more valuable.

00:01:28.558 --> 00:01:29.569
Substitutes get eaten.

00:01:29.569 --> 00:01:33.277
Constraints get repriced: what a ban gives up is worth more

00:01:33.277 --> 00:01:34.625
as the model improves.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.932
To recap: four committed stages, and three kinds of scaffolding.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.030
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:43.610
I'm Mambo.

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:43.900
Bye!
